The mother is **phenotypically normal but a carrier**, so the allele is recessive: a carrier shows the disease only when no normal allele masks it. The disease reaches only the sons and only some of them, so the allele rides on the **X chromosome**:
- the carrier mother is \(\text{X}\text{X}^{\text{d}}\), and each son receives one of her two X chromosomes, so on average half her sons get \(\text{X}^{\text{d}}\);
- a son has a single X chromosome, so the one recessive allele he inherits is expressed and he is affected;
- a daughter also receives a normal X from a normal father, so she is at most a carrier like her mother.

This is the pattern of haemophilia and colour blindness. An autosomal disease would not single out the sons, and a sex-linked dominant allele would make the carrier mother herself diseased. So the answer is option (D) sex-linked recessive.
